      "bodyTr": "Democratic Party Chairman Kim Min-seok officially raised the possibility of impeaching Chief Justice Cho Hee-dae on the 16th, saying \"we have begun discussing all methods and routes.\" Chairman Kim, who has been pressuring the chief justice to voluntarily step down, has now played his last card. It is extraordinarily rare in constitutional history to publicize an impeachment motion against the head of the Supreme Court, one pillar of the separation of powers, and there is deep concern about whether this truly reflects public sentiment. There had been calls for \"Cho Hee-dae's impeachment\" among hard-liners within the Democratic Party on several occasions, but they refrained from acting on it because they recognized the significant risk of backlash. Now they are going public with the argument that grounds for impeachment are abundant. They claim that the refusal to resubmit supreme court justices for reappointment, refusal to take an oath as a witness at parliamentary audits, and misuse of operational funds all constitute impeachment grounds. An impeachment motion against the chief justice passes through the National Assembly plenary session with the support of more than half of the sitting members. The Democratic Party alone can proceed with impeachment. If that happens, Chief Justice Cho's duties would be suspended and the impeachment motion would face a Constitutional Court trial. However, whether the Constitutional Court would uphold the impeachment is a separate matter. For impeachment to be accepted, grave violations of the Constitution or laws must be confirmed, but the consensus among legal experts and scholars is that refusing to resubmit supreme court justices does not qualify as grounds for impeachment. Moreover, many argue that the constitutional basis for resubmission itself remains unclear. If this, as some worry, is a political calculation to resolve internal discord within the ruling camp over matters like the appointment of the new Special Investigation Unit chief and to rally supporters, it is all the more inappropriate. Chairman Kim must first face the reality of his ruling party. Recent polling shows that public sentiment toward the Democratic Party has cooled to the point of falling behind the People Power Party. This erosion of confidence stems from the party's failure to control its track record of vacillating between \"acceleration\" and \"U-turns\" on major policies such as real estate and a comprehensive survey of agricultural land. If formalizing the \"Cho Hee-dae impeachment\" goes beyond using it as pressure for resignation and is intended to force a parliamentary vote to actually suspend his duties, that would be a dangerously reckless idea. How does the party plan to take responsibility for the aftershocks of judicial paralysis that a chief justice vacancy would bring? Chairman Kim should immediately abandon these perilous impeachment discussions that endanger constitutional order. Rather than trying to break through low approval ratings with this extreme gamble, he should prove the ruling party's reason for holding power through tangible achievements in addressing people's livelihoods.",
